<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1744">1744</a>–<a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Queen Charlotte · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mecklenburg">House of Mecklenburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charlotte of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a> (1744–1818) was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> and queen consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and Ireland from 1761. A princess of the small ducal house of Mecklenburg-Strelitz, she bore fifteen children, including the future George IV and William IV. A patron of music and botany, she gave her name to Charlottesville, Charlotte in North Carolina and other places.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlotte-of-mecklenburg-strelitz.jpg" alt="Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Gainsborough · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlotte_gainsborough.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charlotte was born on 19 May 1744, the youngest daughter of Duke Charles Louis Frederick of Mecklenburg, Prince of Mirow. Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a> was a small north-German duchy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. She was taught literature, botany, natural history, French, Italian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and played the harpsichord, though diplomatic reports at the time of her engagement called her education very mediocre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>, unmarried when he succeeded in 1760, needed a wife, and the Hanoverian minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> suggested Charlotte. At 17 she appealed partly because a girl raised in an insignificant duchy would probably have no interest in party politics, and George told her soon after the wedding not to meddle. They married on 8 September 1761. They had 15 children, the first of them the future George IV, born on 12 August 1762, and all but two survived to adulthood. She had a strained relationship with her mother-in-law, Augusta, and turned to German companions.</p><p>She was a music lover and an amateur botanist who enlarged the collections at Kew Gardens, and the bird of paradise flower was named Strelitzia reginae for her. The eight-year-old Mozart played before her in 1764. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> fell ill from October 1788 until March 1789, which distressed her greatly, and after his illness became permanent in 1811 she was made his guardian while her son governed as Prince Regent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charlotte died on 17 November 1818 at Kew Palace and was buried at St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle. She had been queen consort for 57 years and 70 days, the longest of any British queen consort. Places named for her include Charlotte in North Carolina, Charlottesville in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, Charlottetown and the Queen Charlotte Islands, now Haida Gwaii. Her funding kept the General Lying-in Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> open, and it is now Queen Charlotte's and Chelsea Hospital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Britain the German-born Queen <b>Charlotte</b> introduced a tree in 1800, and the custom spread more widely after the marriage of Queen Victoria to Prince Albert.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Christmas tree developed in Central Europe, particularly Germany and Livonia, and was first recorded among German Lutherans in the 16th century; in Britain the German-born Queen <b>Charlotte</b> introduced a tree in 1800, and the custom spread after the marriage of Queen Victoria to Prince Albert.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</b> married George III of Great Britain in 1761, and her niece Louise became Queen of Prussia and a national heroine.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mirow, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.27,12.8&amp;year=1792" aria-label="Mirow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4835 331 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5285 601h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5285 601h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.27,12.8&amp;year=1792">What was here in 1792? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
